1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,720
Hi, we're Keyi, Shengdong and Yilun,
and this is a CIS 5190 Deep Dive.

2
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:13,920
Our question: can you align a language model with
human feedback without any reinforcement learning?

3
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:16,320
Here is the usual recipe.

4
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:22,080
You collect pairs of responses and
ask people which one is better.

5
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:26,400
Stage one trains a reward
model on those choices.

6
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:30,720
Stage two runs reinforcement
learning to earn high reward,

7
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:35,520
with a penalty for drifting
from where the model started.

8
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:41,760
In 2023, a paper called Direct Preference
Optimization showed that you can delete

9
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:45,600
both stages and use a single loss instead.

10
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:51,840
And it is a loss you already know
from this class: logistic regression.

11
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:58,080
This builds on three parts of the course:
logistic regression and maximum likelihood,

12
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:04,800
language models, and the RL module, which
gives us the objective we start from.

13
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:10,560
By the end, you should be
able to derive the DPO loss,

14
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:16,320
explain what beta controls, and
say when DPO has no finite optimum.

15
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:18,240
Start with the data.

16
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:24,000
One prompt, two responses, and a
person picks the one they prefer:

17
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,360
y-w, the winner, and y-l, the loser.

18
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:32,160
The Bradley–Terry model gives
each response a hidden score, r.

19
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:39,360
The probability that the winner is preferred
is the sigmoid of the difference in scores.

20
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:45,120
That is logistic regression, where
the logit is a difference of rewards.

21
00:01:45,120 --> 00:01:50,400
So a reward model is trained with
the usual negative log-likelihood.

22
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:51,840
Remember two things.

23
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:54,240
Only differences of reward matter.

24
00:01:54,240 --> 00:02:01,440
And like any logistic regression, this has
no finite solution if the data are separable.

25
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:02,880
Now stage two.

26
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:07,200
RLHF wants the policy pi that
maximizes expected reward,

27
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:12,480
minus beta times the KL divergence
from a reference model, pi-ref.

28
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:16,800
KL is zero only when the
two distributions match,

29
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:21,600
so a large beta keeps the
policy near the reference.

30
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:24,480
Usually this is optimized with PPO.

31
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:27,360
But it has an exact solution.

32
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:33,600
Write the reward as beta
times the log of e to the r

33
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:37,440
over beta, and fold it into the logarithm.

34
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:43,680
The objective now looks almost like
a KL divergence between pi and pi-ref

35
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:47,040
times e to the r over beta.

36
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:53,280
That does not sum to one,
so we divide by its sum, Z.

37
00:02:53,280 --> 00:02:59,520
What is left is minus beta
times the KL between pi and a

38
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:02,400
new distribution,
pi-star, plus a constant.

39
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:05,760
KL is zero when pi equals pi-star.

40
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:11,520
So the best policy is the reference
model, reweighted by exponentiated reward.

41
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:13,440
There is a catch.

42
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:19,680
Z sums over every possible response,
which is hopeless for a language model.

43
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:22,080
Here is the key move.

44
00:03:22,080 --> 00:03:24,960
Step one: solve for the reward.

45
00:03:24,960 --> 00:03:31,680
It equals beta times the log-ratio of
pi-star to pi-ref, plus beta log Z.

46
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:35,040
So any policy secretly defines a reward.

47
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:41,760
Step two: Bradley–Terry only needs the
difference of two rewards for the same prompt.

48
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:45,120
Both carry the same beta log Z.

49
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:47,520
Subtract, and Z is gone.

50
00:03:47,520 --> 00:03:52,320
Step three: put what is
left into the logistic loss,

51
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:56,640
with our trainable model
pi-theta in place of pi-star.

52
00:03:56,640 --> 00:03:59,040
This is the DPO loss.

53
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:02,400
No reward network, no sampling, no Z.

54
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:08,160
Its gradient pushes the chosen response
up relative to the rejected one,

55
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:12,960
weighted by how badly the model
currently ranks that pair.

56
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:16,800
How close is DPO to the two-stage recipe?

57
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:20,160
In a small problem, they are identical.

58
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:24,480
Take six responses with one
free logit, theta, each.

59
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:28,800
Define r as beta times
theta minus log pi-ref.

60
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:35,040
Then the DPO loss is exactly the Bradley–Terry
loss: same function, different coordinates.

61
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:39,360
My teammate will now test that in a demo.

62
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:49,060
This is our toy language model: six candidate
answers and four hundred noisy preference pairs.

63
00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:56,820
Route 1, in blue, fits Bradley–Terry
rewards and applies the closed-form policy.

64
00:04:56,820 --> 00:05:03,933
Route 2, in orange, is DPO,
trained directly on the logits.

65
00:05:03,933 --> 00:05:05,227
Press play.

66
00:05:05,227 --> 00:05:11,693
They take different paths
and land on the same policy.

67
00:05:11,693 --> 00:05:20,100
The gap between them falls from
about five percent to below ten to

68
00:05:20,100 --> 00:05:25,920
the minus fourteen, and both
gradients are essentially zero.

69
00:05:25,920 --> 00:05:33,033
If we match the step sizes,
using eta times beta squared,

70
00:05:33,033 --> 00:05:37,560
the two paths coincide at every step.

71
00:05:37,560 --> 00:05:41,146
Now look at beta equals one.

72
00:05:41,146 --> 00:05:50,111
Response A has the highest learned reward,
but the most likely answer is still C.

73
00:05:50,111 --> 00:05:50,708
Why?

74
00:05:50,708 --> 00:05:56,087
The policy is the reference
model reweighted by reward,

75
00:05:56,087 --> 00:06:02,661
and the reference gave C
thirty percent and A only five.

76
00:06:02,661 --> 00:06:09,833
At beta equals zero point one, A
jumps to about ninety-eight percent.

77
00:06:09,833 --> 00:06:16,407
At beta equals ten, the policy
is almost exactly the reference.

78
00:06:16,407 --> 00:06:19,395
The reward bars never move.

79
00:06:19,395 --> 00:06:23,579
Beta does not change what is learned.

80
00:06:23,579 --> 00:06:28,360
It changes how strongly we act on it.

81
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:30,417
Now the failure case.

82
00:06:30,417 --> 00:06:35,045
Suppose A beats every other
response, every single time.

83
00:06:35,045 --> 00:06:37,616
This is the separable case.

84
00:06:37,616 --> 00:06:44,816
Raising A's reward always lowers the
loss, so there is no finite best answer.

85
00:06:44,816 --> 00:06:50,473
On noisy data, the reward
margin levels off near one half.

86
00:06:50,473 --> 00:06:58,186
Here it keeps climbing, by about two point
three for every tenfold increase in steps.

87
00:06:58,186 --> 00:07:03,843
Both routes do this, because they
are the same logistic regression.

88
00:07:03,843 --> 00:07:08,471
Azar and co-authors pointed
out this weakness in 2024.

89
00:07:08,471 --> 00:07:11,557
So what does beta do now?

90
00:07:11,557 --> 00:07:15,156
At any fixed step, it still matters.

91
00:07:15,156 --> 00:07:21,327
After ten thousand steps, the
margin is the same for every beta,

92
00:07:21,327 --> 00:07:26,984
but the probability of A is
ninety-nine percent at beta one,

93
00:07:26,984 --> 00:07:31,612
forty-six at beta three,
and eleven at beta ten.

94
00:07:31,612 --> 00:07:37,269
What beta no longer gives
you is a place to stop.

95
00:07:37,269 --> 00:07:43,440
For any fixed beta, the policy keeps
drifting toward always answering A.

96
00:07:43,440 --> 00:07:44,400
Three takeaways.

97
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:49,200
One: learning a reward from pairwise
preferences is logistic regression.

98
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:56,880
Two: the KL-regularized objective has a closed-form answer,
and because preferences are pairwise, the normalizer cancels.

99
00:07:56,880 --> 00:07:59,280
That gives the DPO loss.

100
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:04,560
Three: DPO inherits a failure of
logistic regression on separable data.

101
00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:09,840
Beta controls how far the
policy moves at a given step,

102
00:08:09,840 --> 00:08:13,200
but it cannot supply a finite optimum.

103
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:17,040
Here are two questions to
check your understanding.

104
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:20,400
The answers are in the slide notes.

105
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:21,840
Thanks for watching.
